An ordinary point of inflection has $f'(x) \neq 0$. The gradient can have a local maximum or minimum there; it is not always the steepest point of the curve. For $y = 0.6x^{3} + 0.8x$, the gradient $1.8x^{2} + 0.8$ is locally smallest at the inflection.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.6*\x*\x*\x+0.8*\x});
\draw[dashed] (-1.0,-0.8) -- (1.0,0.8);
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,align=center,text width=3.4cm,anchor=south] at (0,2.5) {Point of\\inflection};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,align=center,text width=3.4cm,anchor=north] at (0,-2.5) {$f''$ changes sign,\\$f' \neq 0$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+4.8},{0.8*\x*\x*\x});
\draw[dashed] (4.3,0) -- (5.3,0);
\fill (4.8,0) circle (2pt);
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,align=center,text width=3.4cm,anchor=south] at (4.8,2.5) {Horizontal\\inflection};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,align=center,text width=3.4cm,anchor=north] at (4.8,-2.5) {$f''$ changes sign,\\$f' = 0$};
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+9.6},{0.9*\x*\x*\x*\x-0.9});
\draw[dashed] (9.1,-0.9) -- (10.1,-0.9);
\fill (9.6,-0.9) circle (2pt);
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,align=center,text width=3.4cm,anchor=south] at (9.6,2.5) {Undulation\\point};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,align=center,text width=3.4cm,anchor=north] at (9.6,-2.5) {$f''$ keeps its sign,\\$f' = 0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=6x^2\\f''(x)&=12x\\g'(x)&=8x^3\\g''(x)&=24x^2\end{aligned}$
| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |
|---|---|---|---|
| $f'(x)$ | $6$ | $0$ | $6$ |
| $f''(x)$ | $-12$ | $0$ | $12$ |
| $g'(x)$ | $-8$ | $0$ | $8$ |
| $g''(x)$ | $24$ | $0$ | $24$ |
For $f$, concavity changes from down to up and $f'(0)=0$. Thus $(0,1)$ is a horizontal point of inflection.
For $g$, concavity stays up while the gradient changes from negative to positive. Thus $(0,1)$ is a very flat local minimum, called an undulation point in the booklet.
